    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of absolute value.\n\nContent:\nLet's first remind ourselves of the rules of absolute value:\n\nContent:\n- [MATH: |{-a}| = |a|]\n- [MATH: |a \\cdot b| = |a| \\cdot |b|]\n- [MATH: \\left| \\dfrac{a}{b} \\right| = \\dfrac{|a|}{|b|}]\n\nContent:\nRecall that the last two rules are known as the distributive properties.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Absolute Value Expressions\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: |{-3z}|-5\\cdot|4z|.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirstly, since [MATH: |{-a}| = |a|,] we can write our expression as\n\n[MATH: |3z|-5\\cdot|4z|.]\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication, and simplify:\n\n[MATH: |3z|\u2212 5 \u22c5|4z||=;|3|\u22c5|z|\u2212 5 \u22c5|4|\u22c5|z||=; 3 \u22c5|z|\u2212 5 \u22c5 4 \u22c5|z||=; 3 \u22c5|z|\u2212 20 \u22c5|z||=; 3|z|\u2212 20|z||=; \u2212 17|z|]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: 4|\u2212 x|\u2212|4x|.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 8|x|]\nb. [MATH: 0]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 8|x|]\nd. [MATH: 8x]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 4|x|]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: |\u2212 5x|\u2212 2 \u22c5|\u2212 2x|.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 9|x|]\nc. [MATH: \u2212|x|]\nd. [MATH: 9|x|]\ne. [MATH: |x|]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Tutorial: The Absolute Value of a Square\n\nContent:\nWe know that the absolute value operation does nothing to a number if it is non-negative. For example:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: |0||= 0;|1||= 1;|2||= 2;|3||= 3; \u22ee]\n\nContent:\nWe can summarize this fact as follows:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: |x| = x \\quad \\text{ if } \\quad x \\geq 0]\n\nContent:\nWe also know that [MATH: x^2] is non-negative for any value of [MATH: x]. Therefore,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\left|x^2\\right| = x^2]\n\nContent:\nwhere [MATH: x] is any real number.\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Simplifying the Absolute Value of a Square\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\left| - \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirstly, since [MATH: |{-a}| = |a|,] we can write our expression as\n\n[MATH: \\left| \\dfrac{x^2}{10} \\right|.]\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n[MATH: |(x^{2})/(10)||=; (|x^{2}|)/(|10|)|=; (|x^{2}|)/(10)]\n\nSince [MATH: x^2 \\geq 0] for any real [MATH: x,] we have that [MATH: \\left|x^2\\right|=x^2.] Therefore,\n\n[MATH: (|x^{2}|)/(10)|= (x^{2})/(10).]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \u2212|4x^{2}|+ x^{2}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 4x^{2}]\nb. [MATH: 5x^{2}]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 5x^{2}]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 3x^{2}]\ne. [MATH: 4x^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: |\u2212 (x^{2})/(8)|=]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (x^{2})/(8)]\nb. [MATH: (x^{2})/(4)]\nc. [MATH: (\u2212 (x)^{2})/(8)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (x^{2})/(8)]\ne. [MATH: 4x^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Tutorial: The Absolute Value of a Negative Variable\n\nContent:\nWe know that [MATH: |x|=x] if [MATH: x \\geq 0.] But what if [MATH: x < 0?]\n\nContent:\nIf a variable is known to be negative, then the absolute value changes it to positive. To turn a negative number into a positive number, we multiply by [MATH: -1.] Therefore, we have\n\nContent:\n[MATH: |x| = -x \\quad\\textrm{for}\\quad x < 0.]\n\nContent:\nTo convince ourselves that this is indeed true, let's verify it in the case when [MATH: x=-2.] Substituting this value into the above, we get\n\nContent:\n[MATH: |\u2212 2||= \u2212 (\u2212 2) = 2.\u2713]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Example: Evaluating an Absolute Value Expression with a Domain Restriction\n\nExample Prompt:\nGiven that [MATH: x\\lt 0,] simplify [MATH: |{-6x}|+2x.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirstly, since [MATH: |{-a}| = |a|,] we can write our expression as\n\n[MATH: |6x|+2x.]\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication. This gives\n\n[MATH: |6x|+ 2x|=;|6|\u22c5|x|+ 2x|=; 6 \u22c5|x|+ 2x|=; 6|x|+ 2x|.]\n\nNow, since [MATH: x \\lt 0,] we have that [MATH: |x|=-x.] Therefore,\n[MATH: 6|x|+ 2x|=; 6(\u2212 x) + 2x|=; \u2212 6x + 2x|=; \u2212 4x|.]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: x \u2265 0,] simplify [MATH: |\u2212 5x|+ 3x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 2x]\nb. [MATH: 3x]\nc. [MATH: 8x]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 6x]\ne. [MATH: 2x]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nGiven that [MATH: x < 0,] simplify [MATH: |\u2212 12x|\u2212 4x.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 16x]\nb. [MATH: 12x]\nc. [MATH: 8x]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 16x]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 12x]"
  }
